PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.2/10 (bar 8), 1 level(s) below 6/10: An unusually well-designed puzzle-style campaign. Every unit has a stated lesson, the verbs arrive one at a time, and breathers and bosses are placed well. The READ rows make the odds transparent and fair. Weak spots: a too-easy Ch1; a steep difficulty cliff in Ch5-6 (teach units at 62-70% mid-skill, about 10 units); repeated fatigue/captain/feint combines in Ch6; thin auction depth; scripted DRS; and little visible career/upgrade/unlock pull between units. Strong, but it needs tuning and a meta layer to match the best in the genre. Curve: Structure is strong: 6 chapters, one verb per unit, a clear teach→twist→combine→spike→breather→boss rhythm, and ghost duels as breathers (21, 32, 54, 63). Problems: (1) Ch1 is too easy and too long. L1-L7 are 90-100% win with near-zero stakes, and L1 is a one-tap trial after a 90 s tutorial. Compress it. (2) The ramp is a cliff, not a curve. Mid-skill win rates fall from ~90% in Ch1-2 to ~62-70% in Ch5-6, and 10 units sit at 62-70% for mid-skill players. Skilled players in the same chapters win 75-85%. That is brutal for a F2P mobile audience. (3) Several 'teach' units are harder than the spikes (L36 69%, L46 65%, L47 65%, L45 62%), and teach units carry 5-10 pts less win rate than their stated purpose allows. A teach unit should be ≥85%. (4) Ch4 mixes the auction and squad systems unevenly. Auction units (34, 41, 42, 44) are the only meta-economy lessons and are ~70% for mid players. The auction is the genre's stickiest hook, and it is buried in 4 units. (5) Difficulty in Ch6 is stacked with the same verbs (fatigue, captain, feints), so bosses L60, L65 and L66 feel like repeats. (6) Retention hooks are described in the loop (ladder, Kit Album, seasons) but the unit list gives no unlock gating or upgrade pull between chapters, and no 'why replay' (star-gated upgrades, daily variants). The list is a puzzle campaign, not a league career. Fix: shorten Ch1, flatten Ch5-6 difficulty by ~8-10 pts, add one full auction→match arc per chapter, and put a visible reward at each chapter end.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L1 (5/10): Trivial: 7 identical rookies, 99% win, 4 optimal moves. Not a decision. → Merge with L2 as one two-step tutorial.
L6 (6/10): The cover-ring lesson is a thin variation on L4: same raider, same holds. → Fold the cover into L4/L7 or mix in toe touches.
L36 (6/10): Hardest 'teach' in the set (69%). 5 raids with a Dubki option confuses range. → Remove Dubki and widen the Anchor range.
L41 (6/10): 69% for mid; the auction is solved by buying the Ankle Corner, and the raider is dull (s 0.12). → Make the opponent hard or hide his style.
L46 (6/10): A teach at 65% mid; chain is not clearly different. → Show chain break cost; make chain stronger.
L47 (6/10): Skilled 67% = unfair for a teach. Dubki beats chain, so it is a guess. → Show the Dubki tell.
L50 (6/10): The DRS tutorial is rigged: 'always wrong' removes real decisions and reads as scripted. → Randomise.
L60 (6/10): 62% mid-skill and very tough; the weakness was already taught in L57. → Lower difficulty.
L65 (6/10): A repeat of L60/L66 at 68% mid. → Remove one system.
L3 (7/10): 'Walk home' is stated as the risk, but the gem difference (18% vs 7%) is easy to read, so it is a no-brainer. → Keep it; raise the walk risk so the Dash trade-off is felt.
L4 (7/10): The wrong hold misses 3 in 4, and 'Hold' hands him the point. The telegraph makes it a pure reaction test with no trade-off. → Add one raider who feints, or make some telegraphs late.
L7 (7/10): The struggle is the best new mechanic, but the unit shows it passively. The 3-caught fail is lenient. → Make 'break free' a required objective.
L10 (7/10): Ch1 spike at 85% for skilled players is not a spike. It has the same toolkit as L9. → Add a Do-or-Die-like pressure tag or a tighter lead; lower to ~75% for mid-skill.
L12 (7/10): Hand vs Toe on an all-T mat is a single obvious answer. → Make 1-2 slots not follow the pattern.
L14 (7/10): Mid-skill win rate is 78%, the lowest in the first half of the game. The goal 'at least one low move' is trivial since Dubki is the only home route. → Cut the wall's hold rates or add a hint.
L21 (7/10): Ghost duel breather: first-time ghost has no personality. Pure score chase can feel like filler. → Show a ghost replay snippet and name the friend.
L25 (7/10): Rush tells 'hop → rush' too clearly, and the 4 raids allow only 2 stops. → Add a decoy raid.
L28 (7/10): Mid-skill 67%, 4 raids and a low-probability finish makes it more luck than learning. → Give 5 raids.
L31 (7/10): Revival spike. The 3-on-mat design is good, but 73% mid with a 'finish within 2' goal is harsh. → Allow within 3.
L34 (7/10): The auction is just a budget sum. 73% mid-skill; the budget solution is fixed. → Add a surprise bid; show the live rival.
L35 (7/10): Specialists' ×1.25 is small; selecting the right defender is a reaction task. → Add a case where the right specialist is out of range.
L38 (7/10): 'practise' that is really a spike. Fail on trailing by 1 is fine. → Re-tag as a test.
L40 (7/10): Pure repeat of the A/T mirror from L16/L13 with high odds against. → Add a unique rule.
L44 (7/10): The boss has an auction tied to a Corner. 72% mid. A Corner requirement feels like a forced buy. → Let any purchase help.
L45 (7/10): 62% mid; the T on the right makes this a one-route puzzle. → Offer two routes.

level-design score 7.2/10 — An unusually well-designed puzzle-style campaign. Every unit has a stated lesson, the verbs arrive one at a time, and breathers and bosses are placed well. The READ rows make the odds transparent and fair. Weak spots: a too-easy Ch1; a steep difficulty cliff in Ch5-6 (teach units at 62-70% mid-skill, about 10 units); repeated fatigue/captain/feint combines in Ch6; thin auction depth; scripted DRS; and little visible career/upgrade/unlock pull between units. Strong, but it needs tuning and a meta layer to match the best in the genre.
curve: Structure is strong: 6 chapters, one verb per unit, a clear teach→twist→combine→spike→breather→boss rhythm, and ghost duels as breathers (21, 32, 54, 63). Problems: (1) Ch1 is too easy and too long. L1-L7 are 90-100% win with near-zero stakes, and L1 is a one-tap trial after a 90 s tutorial. Compress it. (2) The ramp is a cliff, not a curve. Mid-skill win rates fall from ~90% in Ch1-2 to ~62-70% in Ch5-6, and 10 units sit at 62-70% for mid-skill players. Skilled players in the same chapters win 75-85%. That is brutal for a F2P mobile audience. (3) Several 'teach' units are harder than the spikes (L36 69%, L46 65%, L47 65%, L45 62%), and teach units carry 5-10 pts less win rate than their stated purpose allows. A teach unit should be ≥85%. (4) Ch4 mixes the auction and squad systems unevenly. Auction units (34, 41, 42, 44) are the only meta-economy lessons and are ~70% for mid players. The auction is the genre's stickiest hook, and it is buried in 4 units. (5) Difficulty in Ch6 is stacked with the same verbs (fatigue, captain, feints), so bosses L60, L65 and L66 feel like repeats. (6) Retention hooks are described in the loop (ladder, Kit Album, seasons) but the unit list gives no unlock gating or upgrade pull between chapters, and no 'why replay' (star-gated upgrades, daily variants). The list is a puzzle campaign, not a league career. Fix: shorten Ch1, flatten Ch5-6 difficulty by ~8-10 pts, add one full auction→match arc per chapter, and put a visible reward at each chapter end.
